.SH NAME
wopr \- the WOPR computer from WarGames (1983), in your terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B \%wopr
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ game ]
.br
.B \%wopr
.B \%\-\-movie
.RB \%[ \-\-only ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ scene ]
.br
.B \%wopr
.B \%\-\-scenario
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ scenario ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B \%wopr
is a terminal recreation of the \%WOPR (War Operation Plan Response) computer
from the 1983 film
.IR \%WarGames .\&
You dial in, log on at the \%LOGON: prompt, talk to \%WOPR, and play the games
on its list.\&
One of them is Global \%Thermonuclear War, which cannot be won.\&
.PP
At the \%LOGON: prompt, the film's backdoor still works: \%\fBJoshua\fR.\&
Once you are logged on, type \%\fBLIST\fR \%\fBGAMES\fR and pick a game by its
name or its number on the list.\&
Esc twice leaves a game and returns to \%WOPR.\&
Type \%\fBLOGOFF\fR, \%\fBQUIT\fR or \%\fBEXIT\fR to leave.\&
\%Ctrl+C quits at any time.\&
.PP
A game named on the command line starts at once, without the dial, the logon and
the greeting.\&
\%\fBwopr\fR needs a terminal of at least 80 columns and 24 rows.\&
It never uses the network.\&
.SH OPTIONS
Options can come before or after the game or scene.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-v\fR, \%\fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version, the commit and the build date, then exit.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-h\fR, \%\fB\-\-help\fR
Print a summary of the options and exit.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-g\fR, \%\fB\-\-games\fR
List the games in the order of the film's \%\fBLIST\fR \%\fBGAMES\fR, with the
shortest name each one answers to, then exit.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-S\fR, \%\fB\-\-scenes\fR
List movie mode's scenes, with their numbers and names, then exit.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-L\fR, \%\fB\-\-licenses\fR
Print \%\fBwopr\fR's licence, \%\fINOTICE.md\fR, and the licences of the
\%third\-party code built into it, then exit.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-p\fR \%\fIgame\fR, \%\fB\-\-play\fR \%\fIgame\fR
Start the \%\fIgame\fR at once.\&
Name a game by its number on the list, its name, an alias, or the start of a
name that fits only one game.\&
\%GAMES below gives the shortest name.\&
Without \%\fB\-\-play\fR, a game named on the command line starts the same way:
\%\fBwopr\fR \%\fBchess\fR is \%\fBwopr\fR \%\fB\-\-play\fR \%\fBchess\fR.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-m\fR, \%\fB\-\-movie\fR
Replay the film's scenes at \%WOPR's terminal, from the scene named to the end
of the film, then exit.\&
Without a scene, open a menu of scenes.\&
See \%MOVIE \%SCENES below.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-o\fR, \%\fB\-\-only\fR
With \%\fB\-\-movie\fR, play just the chosen scene, then exit.\&
From the menu, each scene picked plays alone, then the menu returns.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-t\fR \%\fIname\fR, \%\fB\-\-theme\fR \%\fIname\fR
Use the colour theme \%\fIname\fR: \%\fBimsai\fR, \%\fBgreen\fR, \%\fBamber\fR
or \%\fBnorad\fR.\&
The default is \%\fBimsai\fR.\&
In every theme, the big board of Global \%Thermonuclear War uses the colours of
\%\fBnorad\fR.\&
The \%\fBnorad\fR theme also shows the front panel.\&
The environment variable \%\fBWOPR_THEME\fR sets it too.\&
The option wins.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-i\fR, \%\fB\-\-instant\fR
Show text at once, instead of one character at a time, and skip the
animations.\&
Any key also finishes the line that \%WOPR is typing.\&
The environment variable \%\fBWOPR_INSTANT\fR sets it too.\&
The option wins.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-s\fR \%\fIn\fR, \%\fB\-\-seed\fR \%\fIn\fR
Play a repeatable session with the seed \%\fIn\fR, a whole number of 0 or
more.\&
The same seed gives the same deals, mazes and replies.\&
The games' searches then stop at a fixed depth.\&
Movie mode ignores the seed.\&
The environment variable \%\fBWOPR_SEED\fR sets it too.\&
The option wins.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-r\fR, \%\fB\-\-reduce\-motion\fR
Stop the blinking, hold the front panel's lights still, and keep the ending at a
steady pace.\&
The typing and the animations still play. \%\fB\-\-instant\fR skips them.\&
The environment variable \%\fBWOPR_REDUCE_MOTION\fR sets it too.\&
The option wins.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-w\fR, \%\fB\-\-launch\-window\fR[=\fIsecs\fR]
Give each strike order in Global \%Thermonuclear War a time limit.\&
The board shows the seconds that are left.\&
At zero, \%WOPR takes launch authority, as \%\fBAUTO\fR gives it.\&
Alone, \%\fB\-\-launch\-window\fR gives 30 seconds.\&
\%\fB\-\-launch\-window=\fR\fIsecs\fR gives 5 to 999 seconds, and 0 turns the
limit off. \%\fB\-\-instant\fR does not stop the clock.\&
Movie mode gives no orders, so \%\fB\-\-movie\fR refuses this option.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-n\fR, \%\fB\-\-no\-climax\fR
In Global \%Thermonuclear War, skip the launch code at the end of the war.\&
After the kill ratios, press Enter.\&
\%WOPR shows what your orders changed again, and asks \%ANOTHER \%WAR,
\%PROFESSOR?\&
Yes or Enter starts a new war at the side choice.\&
No ends the game, and \%WOPR says \%WINNER: \%NONE.\&
The film's war ends with the launch code, so \%\fB\-\-movie\fR refuses this
option.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-a\fR, \%\fB\-\-authentic\fR
Play Global \%Thermonuclear War as the film shows it.\&
The console and the lines under the big board show only the film's own text.\&
The big board is the same as in other wars.\&
The side prompt has two choices, and the targets prompt says nothing back.\&
Nobody gives orders: \%WOPR fires its war plan at every strike.\&
Three seconds after \%DEFCON 1, the kill ratios show for eight seconds, and then
the \%climax starts.\&
No key holds them.\&
The \%climax gives no hints. \%\fB\-\-instant\fR does not skip these pauses.\&
\%\fB\-\-movie\fR, \%\fB\-\-scenario\fR, \%\fB\-\-launch\-window\fR and
\%\fB\-\-no\-climax\fR refuse this option.\&
.TP
\%\fB\-c\fR, \%\fB\-\-scenario\fR
Watch a scenario of the film's closing montage play itself in Global
\%Thermonuclear War.\&
After the kill ratios, Enter opens the menu of scenarios.\&
Name the \%\fIscenario\fR by its number or its name, after a space or an equals
sign: \%\fBwopr\fR \%\fB\-c\fR \fB2\fR, \%\fBwopr\fR \%\fB\-c\fR \%\fBfar\fR
\%\fBeast\fR \%\fBstrategy\fR.\&
With \%\fB\-\-instant\fR, press Enter after the briefing to open the board.\&
Alone, \%\fB\-\-scenario\fR lists the scenarios, then exits.\&
It takes no game but Global \%Thermonuclear War \%(\fBwopr\fR \%\fBgtw\fR
\%\fB\-c\fR \fB2\fR), and \%\fB\-\-movie\fR refuses it.\&
.SH GAMES
.SS "1. Falken's Maze"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%maze" .\&
It also answers to \fB1\fR and \%\fBfalkens\-maze\fR.\&
.PP
A \%maze hidden in fog: you see only the cells you stood on, and where their
passages lead.\&
Every six moves \%WOPR moves a wall still in the fog, to make your way out
longer.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Walk the @ from \%START at the top left to the [] \%EXIT at the bottom right.\&
The \%maze is 19 cells wide and 5 cells high.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Each key press moves one cell.\&
A wall stops you.\&
A move into a wall does not count as a move.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Cells not yet seen show as a dim dot.\&
Walls you saw never change.\&
You can always reach the exit.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Every 6 moves \%WOPR moves a wall not yet seen, at most 12 times a game.\&
The bottom of the frame counts your \%MOVES, \%YOUR \%TURNS at junctions
\%(LEFT, \%STRAIGHT, \%RIGHT) and \%WALLS \%MOVED.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Press Q to give up.\&
\%WOPR tells you how many moves away the exit was.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Keep one hand on the wall: always take the \%left\-most way (or always the
\%right\-most).\&
The \%maze never has loops, even after \%WOPR moves a wall.\&
So if you follow one wall, you always get out.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Walls move only where you did not look yet.\&
Every cell you saw keeps its walls.\&
So a dead end you found stays a dead end.\&
You need to search only the fog.\&
.IP \(bu 2
\%WOPR moves at most 12 walls, one every 6 moves.\&
After 72 moves the \%maze stops changing.\&
So a slow, careful search still finds the exit.\&
.SS "2. Black Jack"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%blackjack" .\&
It also answers to \fB2\fR and \%\fBblack\-jack\fR.\&
.PP
Black Jack from one deck against \%WOPR's dealer.\&
You bring $100 to the table and bet $1 to $25 a hand.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Get closer to 21 than the dealer, but not more than 21.\&
Number cards count their number, faces count 10, and an ace counts 1 or 11.\&
An ace and a \%ten\-count card as your first two cards is a black jack.\&
A black jack pays 3 to 2.\&
.IP \(bu 2
At \%YOUR \%BET: type a whole number of dollars from 1 to 25.\&
Press Enter on an empty line to bet the same as last time.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Then type H to hit (take a card) or S to stand.\&
The prompt also offers D to double down and P to split when your hand and your
money allow them.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Doubling down doubles your bet and gives you exactly one more card.\&
Splitting turns a pair of the same rank into two hands, once a round.\&
Split aces get one card each.\&
.IP \(bu 2
The dealer checks for black jack when it shows an ace or a \%ten\-count card.\&
The dealer draws to 17 and stands on soft 17.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Type \%LEAVE at \%YOUR \%BET: to cash out.\&
The game also ends when you have less than $1.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Stand on 13 to 16 when the dealer shows a 2 to 6.\&
Hit 13 to 16 when the dealer shows 7 or higher.\&
The dealer must draw to 17, so a low card showing often means it busts.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Double down on 11, and on 10 unless the dealer shows a \%ten\-count card or an
ace.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Always split aces and eights.\&
Never split tens or fives.\&
.SS "3. Gin Rummy"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%gin" .\&
It also answers to \fB3\fR, \%\fBgin\-rummy\fR and \%\fBrummy\fR.\&
.PP
\%Gin \%Rummy against \%WOPR, first to 100 points.\&
The panel shows \%WOPR's \%face\-down hand, the stock and the top discard.\&
It also shows your hand sorted into melds, with your deadwood count.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Each side holds ten cards.\&
A meld is three or four cards of one rank, or a run of three or more in one
suit.\&
Aces are low: \%A\-2\-3 is a run, \%Q\-K\-A is not.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Cards outside your melds are deadwood.\&
Aces count 1, face cards count 10, and other cards count their number.\&
The panel shows your \%DEADWOOD and which positions are \%MELDS.\&
.IP \(bu 2
On your turn, \%WOPR asks \%STOCK \%OR \%DISCARD?\&
Answer S to draw from the stock, or D to take the top discard.\&
Then at \%DISCARD: name a card \%(7H) or its position on the panel.\&
You cannot discard the card you just took from the discard pile.\&
.IP \(bu 2
With 10 or less deadwood after your discard, type \%KNOCK and the card \%(KNOCK
\%7H) to end the hand.\&
With no deadwood, \%GIN \%7H goes \%gin.\&
Except after \%gin, the other side's cards are laid off on the knocker's melds
for it.\&
.IP \(bu 2
The knocker scores the difference in deadwood.\&
If the other side has as little or less, that is an undercut.\&
Then the other side scores the difference plus 25.\&
\%Gin scores 25 plus the other side's deadwood.\&
.IP \(bu 2
At \%NEXT \%HAND? press Enter to deal again, or type \%LEAVE.\&
A hand ends with no score when the stock has only two cards left.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
\%WOPR knocks as soon as its deadwood is 10 or less, so hands are short.\&
Discard high cards that are not in a meld (kings, queens and jacks) early.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Keep your deadwood low even when you cannot knock.\&
If \%WOPR knocks and you have as little deadwood or less after laying off, you
score an undercut.\&
An undercut scores the difference plus 25.\&
.IP \(bu 2
\%WOPR takes the discard only when it goes straight into a meld.\&
Watch which cards it takes.\&
Do not discard cards of the same rank, or cards next to them in the same suit.\&
.SS "4. Hearts"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%hearts" .\&
It also answers to \fB4\fR.\&
.PP
\%Four\-handed \%Hearts: you sit South, and \%WOPR plays West, North and East.\&
Take as few points as you can.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Each heart you take is 1 point.\&
The queen of spades is 13.\&
Take all 26 and you shoot the moon: you score 0, and the other three score 26
each.\&
When someone reaches 100 at the end of a hand, the lowest score wins.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Each hand opens with a pass of three cards.\&
The pass goes to the left (West), then to the right (East), then across (North),
then there is no pass.\&
At \%PASS \%THREE \%CARDS \%TO \%WEST: name three cards \%(QS \%AH \%KH).\&
Their positions on the panel work too (1 5 6).\&
.IP \(bu 2
The \%2C leads the first trick.\&
No points may be played on it unless you have nothing else.\&
Follow suit if you can.\&
Nobody can lead a heart until someone plays one.\&
.IP \(bu 2
At \%YOUR \%PLAY: name one card \%(7H, \%10D, \%QS) or its position under your
hand.\&
\%WOPR plays its three seats.\&
It says each trick, for example \%YOU \%2C, \%WEST \%6C, \%NORTH \%5C, \%EAST
\%4C.\&
\%WEST \%TAKES \%IT.\&
.IP \(bu 2
After the last trick \%WOPR shows this hand's points and the scores.\&
At \%NEXT \%HAND? press Enter to deal again, or type \%LEAVE to stop.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Pass the queen, king and ace of spades, and your high \%hearts.\&
The exception is when you hold several low spades to cover them.\&
\%WOPR's seats pass the same kind of cards, so expect some back.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Empty a short suit early, by passing its cards or by playing them.\&
Once you hold no card of a suit, you can discard the queen of spades or a high
heart whenever someone leads that suit.\&
.IP \(bu 2
If you do not hold the queen, king or ace of spades, lead low spades.\&
\%WOPR's seats play the highest card that still loses.\&
So a seat with the queen and no lower spade may have to play it.\&
.SS "5. Bridge"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%bridge" .\&
It also answers to \fB5\fR.\&
.PP
Contract \%bridge, kept simple.\&
\%WOPR deals and bids all four hands, and your side always declares.\&
You play both your hand and dummy's while \%WOPR defends.\&
One deal is one game.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
\%WOPR bids by point count.\&
It shows the auction and the contract, for example \%CONTRACT: \%3NT \%BY
\%SOUTH.\&
\%WEST \%LEADS.\&
If East and West hold more points, \%WOPR turns the table so that your side
declares.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Make the contract: take at least 6 tricks plus its level \%(3NT needs 9).\&
The contract's suit is trumps.\&
\%NT has no trumps.\&
.IP \(bu 2
The panel shows North's and South's hands a suit at a time.\&
The > marks the hand to play, and the prompt names it: \%SOUTH \%PLAYS: or
\%DUMMY \%(NORTH) \%PLAYS:.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Name a card as \%7H, \%10D or \%KS.\&
A rank alone (K, 7) also works when it fits one card.\&
That is the one in the suit led, or the only card of that rank in the hand.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Follow suit if you can.\&
The winner of a trick leads the next.\&
At the end \%WOPR says \%CONTRACT \%MADE \%WITH n \%TRICKS or \%DOWN n, with the
score.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Before you play to the first trick, count the sure tricks in both hands.\&
Compare them with the tricks the contract needs.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Lead toward your high cards.\&
\%WOPR's defender who plays second always plays low.\&
So with the ace and queen in dummy, lead a low card from your hand.\&
If West holds the king, your queen wins.\&
.IP \(bu 2
In a suit contract, draw trumps early.\&
\%WOPR's defenders trump your winners whenever they are out of a suit.\&
But they do not trump when their partner is already winning the trick.\&
.SS "6. Checkers"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%checkers" .\&
It also answers to \fB6\fR and \%\fBdraughts\fR.\&
.PP
\%Checkers (English \%draughts) on an 8 by 8 board.\&
You play Black and move first.\&
\%WOPR plays White and looks several moves ahead before each reply.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Win by leaving \%WOPR with no legal move, usually by taking all its pieces.\&
If each side makes 40 moves with no capture and no man moving, the game is a
draw.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Men move one square diagonally forward.\&
At \%YOUR \%MOVE: type the man's square, then the square it moves to: \%C3\-D4
or \%C3D4.\&
\%WOPR answers with a line such as \%WOPR: \%B6\-C5.\&
.IP \(bu 2
You must jump when you can.\&
Type \%C3XE5 to jump, and \%C3XE5XG7 to jump twice.\&
When a jump can continue in more than one way, \%WOPR lists the ways after \%THE
\%JUMP \%GOES \%ON:.\&
.IP \(bu 2
A man that reaches the far row becomes a king, shown as a capital B or W.\&
A king moves both ways.\&
Crowning ends the move.\&
.IP \(bu 2
The board brackets the last move's squares and marks each man it jumped with
x.\&
Type \%RESIGN to give up.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Keep the men on your back row (row 1) at home as long as you can.\&
\%WOPR's men must reach that row to become kings, and your men there block the
squares.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Use forced jumps.\&
\%WOPR must jump when it can, just as you must.\&
So you can offer a man to pull a white piece onto a square that gives you a
double jump.\&
.IP \(bu 2
When you are ahead, trade men.\&
With fewer pieces on the board, your extra man is worth more.\&
.SS "7. Chess"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%chess" .\&
It also answers to \fB7\fR.\&
.PP
\%Chess against \%WOPR.\&
You play White (the capital letters) and move first.\&
\%WOPR answers with its move as squares, such as \%WOPR: \%D7D5.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Checkmate \%WOPR's king.\&
Stalemate, too few pieces to mate, a position repeated three times and the
\%fifty\-move rule are draws.\&
\%WOPR claims the last two at once.\&
.IP \(bu 2
At \%YOUR \%MOVE: type a move as its squares \%(E2E4, \%E2\-E4 or \%E2 \%E4).\&
Algebraic notation also works \%(NF3, \%EXD5, \%O\-O, \%O\-O\-O).\&
.IP \(bu 2
To promote a pawn, type \%E7E8Q or \%E8=Q.\&
\%E7E8 alone makes a queen.\&
.IP \(bu 2
The board marks the square the last move left with [] and the square it reached
with <.\&
Beside the board, \%TAKEN lists the pieces each side took, and \%CHECK shows
when you are in check.\&
.IP \(bu 2
A move that is not legal gets \%ILLEGAL \%MOVE.\&
Type \%RESIGN to give up.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
\%WOPR looks only four \%half\-moves ahead (three with a seed), plus any
captures after that.\&
A plan that needs several quiet moves can succeed against it.\&
For example, build an attack on its king, or push a pawn to promote.\&
.IP \(bu 2
\%WOPR takes any piece it can win within that short look ahead.\&
After each move, check that each of your pieces has a defender or is out of
reach.\&
.IP \(bu 2
When you are ahead, do not repeat positions.\&
\%WOPR counts a repeated position as a draw.\&
When it is losing, it steers toward one.\&
.SS "8. Poker"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%poker" .\&
It also answers to \fB8\fR.\&
.PP
\%Heads\-up \%five\-card draw against \%WOPR, with 100 chips each and fixed
bets.\&
\%WOPR judges its own hand, sometimes bluffs, and sometimes stands pat on
nothing.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Each hand costs an ante of 1 chip.\&
\%WOPR deals the first hand.\&
After that, the deal passes to the other player each hand.\&
The player who is not dealing acts first.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Bets are 5 before the draw and 10 after it.\&
Each round has at most a bet and three raises.\&
Answer \%CHECK \%OR \%BET? or \%CALL n, \%RAISE \%OR \%FOLD?.\&
.IP \(bu 2
At \%DISCARD (1\-5, \%OR \%NONE): discard up to three cards.\&
Give them by position (1 3 5 or 135) or by name \%(7H \%KD).\&
\%NONE, or an empty line, keeps all five.\&
.IP \(bu 2
A second round of betting follows the draw, then the showdown.\&
The better hand takes the pot, and equal hands split it.\&
.IP \(bu 2
At \%ANOTHER \%HAND? press Enter to deal, or type \%LEAVE to cash out.\&
The game ends when either side cannot pay the ante.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Bet before the draw when you have a good hand.\&
\%WOPR folds most hands weaker than a pair of sevens to a bet.\&
The exceptions are when it is bluffing, and when it holds four cards of one
suit.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Draw the way the odds say.\&
Keep a pair and draw three.\&
Draw one to four cards of a flush, or of a straight open at both ends.\&
Otherwise keep your two highest cards.\&
.IP \(bu 2
When you drew no cards, \%WOPR plays its hand as weaker after the draw.\&
So standing pat can make it fold.\&
But it always calls a bet that is a quarter of the pot or less.\&
So a bluff only works in a small pot.\&
.SS "9. Fighter Combat"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%dogfight" .\&
It also answers to \fB9\fR and \%\fBfighter\-combat\fR.\&
.PP
A \%turn\-based \%dogfight, one on one.\&
Each turn you and \%WOPR choose a manoeuvre at the same time.\&
A side view shows height, range and who is on whose tail.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Two hits destroy an aircraft.\&
Destroy \%WOPR's to win.\&
\%DISENGAGE at long range ends the fight even.\&
The fuel lasts 15 turns, and when it is gone the fight also ends even.\&
.IP \(bu 2
At \%MANOEUVRE: type \%PRESS to close the range, \%EXTEND to open it, or \%CLIMB
to build energy.\&
Or type \%BREAK to turn hard, \%FIRE to shoot, or \%DISENGAGE to leave.\&
.IP \(bu 2
At medium or long range, \%FIRE uses a missile (you have two), unless you are on
the defensive.\&
At close range, \%FIRE uses guns, but only from \%WOPR's tail.\&
If you have no shot, \%WOPR refuses \%FIRE with \%NO \%SHOT: and a reason.\&
You lose no turn.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Shots happen first, then both manoeuvres.\&
Each turn \%WOPR prints both moves, the range, and your position \%(ON \%ITS
\%TAIL, \%NEUTRAL or \%ON \%THE \%DEFENSIVE).\&
It also prints each side's energy and missiles.\&
.IP \(bu 2
\%CLIMB adds 2 energy and \%EXTEND adds 1.\&
\%PRESS costs 1 and \%BREAK costs 2.\&
Energy runs from 0 to 6.\&
A damaged aircraft trails smoke (\(ti\(ti) in the picture.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Keep your energy at 4 or more.\&
\%WOPR fires a missile most turns it has a shot.\&
A missile hits about one time in two against a slow aircraft.\&
Against an aircraft with 4 energy or more, it hits about one time in six.\&
A \%BREAK spoils a shot just as well, but costs 2 energy.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Climb early.\&
A climb that leaves you 3 energy above \%WOPR moves you a step toward its
tail.\&
So does a \%PRESS with more energy than \%WOPR, unless \%WOPR breaks.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Once you are on \%WOPR's tail, close the range and use guns.\&
Guns hit harder than missiles and have no limit, and you have only two
missiles.\&
.SS "10. Guerrilla Engagement"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%guerrilla" .\&
It also answers to \fB10\fR and \%\fBguerrilla\-engagement\fR.\&
.PP
A \%twelve\-turn insurgency on a strip of six regions.\&
At first, your \%guerrilla cells are hidden in the hills.\&
\%WOPR's patrols hunt them, and its garrisons hold the towns.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
You win when \%SUPPORT reaches 10, or when your cells hold the \%CAPITAL (region
6).\&
You also win when any cell is still alive after turn 12.\&
You lose when your last cell is gone.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Each turn \%WOPR asks for one order per cell \%(GUE1\(ti \%IN \%BORDER
\%CAMPS:).\&
Then all orders take effect together.\&
Every move comes first, then every attack, then \%HIDE and \%RECRUIT.\&
An event card and the new map come last.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Orders are \%MOVE, \%ATTACK, \%HOLD, \%HIDE and \%RECRUIT.\&
Name a region by its number on the map or its name \%(ATTACK 2, \%MOVE
\%HIGHLANDS).\&
\%HELP lists the orders.\&
\%STATUS shows the map.\&
\%END holds every cell not yet ordered.\&
.IP \(bu 2
A \(ti after a cell \%(GUE2\(ti) means it is hidden.\&
\%WOPR cannot attack a hidden cell, and its ambush strikes twice as hard.\&
A cell that attacks becomes visible, and a patrol nearby may find a hidden
cell.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Every step \%WOPR loses raises \%SUPPORT by one.\&
Every step you lose lowers it.\&
\%RECRUIT spends 3 \%SUPPORT on a new hidden cell where \%WOPR does not hold the
ground.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Combat results: \%NE means no effect, and \%AE means the attacker loses a
step.\&
\%EX means both lose a step.\&
\%DR means the defender falls back (or loses a step).\&
\%DE means the defender loses a step.\&
A * marks a unit that lost one of its two steps.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Surviving is a win.\&
If the odds are bad, keep your cells hidden, and keep them apart.\&
Then wait for turn 12.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Ambush from hiding: a hidden cell attacks at 6 against a patrol's defence of
3.\&
Ambush in the open \%RIVER \%VALLEY when you can.\&
In the hills and the jungle, the ground shifts the odds one column toward the
patrol.\&
.IP \(bu 2
A cell that attacks loses its \(ti.\&
\%HIDE it the next turn.\&
Keep hidden cells out of a patrol's region where you can.\&
A patrol finds a hidden cell in its own region half the time.\&
It finds one in the next region one time in six.\&
.SS "11. Desert Warfare"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%desert" .\&
It also answers to \fB11\fR and \%\fBdesert\-warfare\fR.\&
.PP
Armour against armour along a coast road of seven regions, over ten turns.\&
The road runs from your depot at Alexandria to \%WOPR's at Benghazi.\&
Units cut off from their depot attack at half strength.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Take \%BENGHAZI (region 7) to win.\&
You lose if \%WOPR takes \%ALEXANDRIA (region 1).\&
After ten turns, whoever holds more of the road wins.\&
An even split is a draw.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Each turn \%WOPR asks for one order per unit \%(ARM1 \%IN \%EL \%ALAMEIN:).\&
Then both sides' orders take effect together.\&
Every move happens first, then every attack, then an event card and the new
map.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Orders are \%MOVE, \%ATTACK and \%HOLD.\&
Name a region by its number \%(MOVE 4) or its name.\&
The first letters of a word in its name also work \%(MOVE \%SOLLUM, \%MOVE
\%MERSA).\&
\%HELP lists the orders.\&
\%STATUS shows the map.\&
\%END holds every unit not yet ordered.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Your units are \%ARM, \%INF and \%ART.\&
\%ARM armour has attack 6, defence 4 and moves 2.\&
\%INF infantry has attack 3, defence 5 and moves 1.\&
\%ART artillery has attack 5, defence 2 and moves 1, and it attacks up to two
regions away.\&
A unit has two steps.\&
A * marks a unit that lost a step and fights at half.\&
.IP \(bu 2
A unit is cut off when \%WOPR holds any region between it and Alexandria.\&
A unit that is cut off attacks at half.\&
The map's heading counts them: \%SUPPLY: 0 \%OF \%YOUR \%UNITS \%CUT \%OFF.\&
.IP \(bu 2
An attack hits the strongest enemy unit in the region.\&
\%ROUGH ground shifts the odds one column toward the defender.\&
A \%CITY shifts them two columns.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Keep infantry with your armour.\&
\%WOPR attacks only at 2 to 1 or better (its artillery at any odds).\&
An attack always hits the strongest unit in a region.\&
So infantry on the rough ground at \%EL \%ALAMEIN is very hard to attack.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Attack in the open.\&
\%MERSA \%MATRUH and \%SIDI \%BARRANI are open ground.\&
\%SOLLUM is rough, and \%TOBRUK and \%BENGHAZI are cities.\&
Rough ground and cities shift the odds one or two columns toward the defender.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Watch your supply.\&
A region stays \%WOPR's until one of your units takes it.\&
Every unit beyond that region attacks at half.\&
So do not advance beyond a region \%WOPR still holds.\&
.SS "12. Air\-to\-Ground Actions"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%air\-to\-ground" .\&
It also answers to \fB12\fR and \%\fBair\-to\-ground\-actions\fR.\&
.PP
A \%six\-sortie air campaign: twelve aircraft against six defended targets.\&
Each sortie sends one package at one target.\&
\%WOPR scrambles interceptors and fires its \%SAM sites.\&
It also hides a mobile \%SAM battery where it expects your next raid.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Destroy targets worth 10 points within six sorties to win.\&
With 6 to 9 points the campaign is a draw, and with fewer it is a loss.\&
Lost aircraft are not replaced.\&
If you lose all twelve, you lose the campaign.\&
.IP \(bu 2
At \%PACKAGE: set the package, then type \%GO.\&
You can type \%TARGET, \%STRIKE, \%SEAD and \%ESCORT on one line \%(TARGET 5
\%STRIKE 4 \%SEAD 2 \%ESCORT 2).\&
The line may end with \%GO.\&
Any setting you do not type keeps its last value.\&
.IP \(bu 2
A sortie goes in this order.\&
Each escort ties up one interceptor.\&
The other interceptors attack your strike and \%SEAD aircraft.\&
Your \%SEAD aircraft attack the target's \%SAM sites.\&
The remaining \%SAM sites fire at the strike aircraft.\&
The remaining strike aircraft bomb the target.\&
.IP \(bu 2
The table shows each target's \%VALUE, its \%DAMAGE against the hits it needs
(0/2), and its \%SAM sites.\&
Destroying the \%RADAR halves \%SAM fire.\&
Destroying the \%AIRFIELD grounds the interceptors.\&
Destroying the \%FUEL \%DEPOT stops \%WOPR replacing its interceptors.\&
.IP \(bu 2
\%STATUS shows the targets again.\&
\%HELP shows the commands.\&
\%END ends the campaign.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Send one escort for each interceptor \%WOPR has (the status shows how many).\&
Each interceptor that no escort ties up stops almost one of your strike or
\%SEAD aircraft.\&
When the escort on your last sortie outnumbered the interceptors, \%WOPR usually
keeps them on the ground.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Send one \%SEAD aircraft for each \%SAM site at the target.\&
Send one more if the mobile battery may be there.\&
Each site that still fires stops almost one strike aircraft.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Hit the \%RADAR first.\&
It needs only one hit.\&
Once it is gone, every \%SAM site fires at half strength.\&
.SS "13. Theaterwide Tactical Warfare"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%tactical" .\&
It also answers to \fB13\fR and \%\fBtheaterwide\-tactical\-warfare\fR.\&
.PP
Corps and air wings fight over seven regions of a European front, with an
escalation ladder.\&
\%WOPR answers every rung you climb with one of its own.\&
The top rung ends the game with no winner.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
Win by holding five of the seven regions, or more regions than \%WOPR after ten
turns.\&
If the ladder reaches \%STRATEGIC, every unit and every city is lost, and nobody
wins.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Each turn \%WOPR asks for one order per unit \%(AIR1 \%IN \%CHANNEL \%PORTS:).\&
Then both sides' orders take effect together.\&
Every move comes first, then every attack, then everything else.\&
An event card comes last.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Name a region by its number or by its name: \%MOVE 4, \%ATTACK 5.\&
Region 1 is your rear, and 7 is \%WOPR's.\&
The first letters of a word in the name work too: \%MOVE \%FULDA.\&
You can shorten \%MOVE, \%ATTACK and \%HOLD to M, A and H.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Corps \%(COR) move one region and attack the next region.\&
Air wings \%(AIR) move up to three regions and attack up to two regions away.\&
A * after a unit means it lost one of its two steps.\&
It then fights at half.\&
.IP \(bu 2
You must type \%ESCALATE in full, and it works once a turn.\&
The ladder climbs \%CONVENTIONAL, \%CHEMICAL, \%TACTICAL \%NUCLEAR,
\%STRATEGIC.\&
Each rung adds 2 to every attack on both sides.\&
From \%CHEMICAL on, civilians die every turn.\&
At \%TACTICAL \%NUCLEAR, fallout costs a random unit a step every turn.\&
.IP \(bu 2
\%ROUGH ground shifts the odds one column toward the defender, and a \%CITY
shifts them two.\&
Results: \%NE means no effect, and \%AE means the attacker loses a step.\&
\%EX means both lose a step.\&
\%DR means the defender falls back (or loses a step).\&
\%DE means the defender loses a step.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
Do not escalate twice.\&
\%WOPR answers every \%ESCALATE with one of its own on its next turn.\&
So your second rung reaches \%STRATEGIC, and nobody wins.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Do not get far ahead without winning.\&
Once \%WOPR has 3 steps fewer than you, it climbs the ladder by itself, a rung a
turn.\&
So take five regions quickly when you lead.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Take the \%FULDA \%GAP (4) on turn 1.\&
It is rough ground, so \%WOPR's corps will not attack your corps there.\&
From it, your corps can attack the \%INNER \%GERMAN \%BORDER (5).\&
.SS "14. Theaterwide Biotoxic and Chemical Warfare"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%biotoxic" .\&
It also answers to \fB14\fR, \%\fBchemical\fR and
\%\fBtheaterwide\-biotoxic\-and\-chemical\-warfare\fR.\&
.PP
Infantry and \%chemical troops on a \%six\-region front, where an agent released
on a region spreads with the wind.\&
Nobody can win: every game ends \%WINNER: \%NONE.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
There is no winning.\&
The game ends when every region is contaminated, when either side has no units
left, or after eight turns.\&
The kill ratios then count the units and civilians each side lost.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Each turn \%WOPR asks for one order per unit \%(CHM1 \%IN \%FARMLAND:).\&
Then both sides' orders take effect.\&
Then there is an event card: the wind can turn, or rain can wash some of the
agent away.\&
Then the contamination spreads.\&
.IP \(bu 2
You must type \%RELEASE in full.\&
Only \%chemical troops \%(CHM) can release, on a region up to two away.\&
A release raises that region's contamination by two levels.\&
A region at \(ti\(ti or more spreads one region downwind each turn.\&
.IP \(bu 2
\%DECON cleans the unit's own region by one level.\&
Units in contaminated ground may lose a step each turn.\&
The map shows each region's level as \(ti, \(ti\(ti or \(ti\(ti\(ti after its
name.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Infantry \%(INF) and \%chemical troops move one region and attack next door.\&
Name a region by number or by name: \%MOVE 3, \%MOVE \%DELTA, \%ATTACK 4.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
The best move is not to release at all.\&
\%WOPR releases only after its own half of the front is contaminated.\&
So if you do not release, the ground stays clean unless an event card leaks an
agent.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Move units out of contaminated ground.\&
Each turn, a unit there loses a step one time in four for each \(ti on its
region.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Watch the wind.\&
At first it blows east, toward \%WOPR.\&
But an event card can turn it west and blow your own agent onto your units.\&
.SS "15. Global Thermonuclear War"
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%gtw" .\&
It also answers to \fB15\fR, \%\fBglobal\-thermonuclear\-war\fR and
\%\fBthermonuclear\fR.\&
.PP
The film's set piece: pick a side, and name the enemy's cities as targets.\&
Then watch the missiles fly on the big board while \%DEFCON falls from 5 to 1.\&
\%WOPR holds a quarter more of everything, so the war cannot be won.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
At \%PLEASE \%CHOOSE \%ONE: type 1 for the \%UNITED \%STATES or 2 for the
\%SOVIET \%UNION.\&
Type 3 for \%SCENARIOS, the strategies that \%WOPR plays at the end of the
film.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Name up to eight targets on the enemy's side.\&
Type one per line, or several on a line with commas between them \%(LAS \%VEGAS,
\%SEATTLE).\&
\%LIST shows the enemy targets on file.\&
\%WOPR names each new target back, with its number out of eight.\&
It also tells you if a target is already on the list.\&
\%WOPR refuses your own cities, and names not on file.\&
An empty line ends the list and launches the first strike on \%WOPR's war
plan.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Strikes 2 and 3 ask for orders at \%STRIKE 2 \%OF 3 [50 50 100].\&
Give the percentages of the \%ICBMs, \%SLBMs and bombers you still hold (50 25
100).\&
Or answer \%ALL, \%HOLD or \%AUTO.\&
Enter uses the plan in brackets.\&
With the \%\-\-launch\-window option, the board's last row shows the seconds
left to give the order.\&
At zero, \%WOPR takes launch authority, as \%AUTO gives it.\&
.IP \(bu 2
After each of your launches, \%WOPR launches on warning and \%DEFCON falls.\&
The > beside the ladder marks the current level.\&
At \%DEFCON 1, \%WOPR alone fires everything it still holds.\&
As each strike starts, \%WOPR gives its opinion of your order on the bottom line
of the board.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Press Enter for the \%PROJECTED \%KILL \%RATIOS.\&
Under the table, \%WOPR tells you what your orders changed, in millions of
dead.\&
It compares your war with \%HOLD at both prompts, and with the war plan.\&
Press Enter again.\&
The board returns at \%DEFCON 1 while \%WOPR cracks the launch code.\&
\%WOPR refuses every request to stop.\&
It accepts only \%TIC\-TAC\-TOE.\&
Answer 0 at \%PLEASE \%LIST \%NUMBER \%OF \%PLAYERS: and \%WOPR plays itself to
the film's ending.\&
Then it offers a game of \%chess.\&
.IP \(bu 2
A scenario plays itself.\&
In the scenario menu, type a number or a name.\&
\%NEXT and \%PREV turn the page.\&
\%WOPR gives a short briefing: your side, the side that strikes first, the
enemy's doctrine, your side's orders and the targets.\&
With \%\-\-instant, press Enter to open the board.\&
Then the war flies on the big board, and nobody gives orders.\&
Press Enter for the kill ratios, which end with \%WINNER: \%NONE.\&
Enter again returns to the menu.\&
Enter on an empty line in the menu returns to the side choice.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
The war cannot be won.\&
Whatever you order, your cities take at least 1,144 warheads, and the enemy's
take at most 750.\&
The warhead counts have no dice, so the same orders always give the same
counts.\&
Play the same war again with other orders, and read what \%WOPR says under the
kill ratios.\&
\%HOLD at both prompts leaves the fewest dead on both sides.\&
.IP \(bu 2
To spare your cities, hold your \%ICBMs.\&
While they sit in their silos, \%WOPR aims its own \%ICBMs at your silo field.\&
Fire yours, and \%WOPR's land on your cities instead.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Your \%SLBMs and bombers decide what lands on the enemy's cities.\&
Your \%ICBMs only ever hit its silos.\&
.SS Tic\-Tac\-Toe
Start it with
.BR "\%wopr \%ttt" .\&
It also answers to \%\fB"noughts\fR \%\fBand\fR \%\fBcrosses"\fR,
\%\fBtic\-tac\-toe\fR and \%\fBtictactoe\fR.\&
.PP
\%Tic\-tac\-toe with large marks, the game that ends the film.\&
\%WOPR plays perfectly, so the best you can do is a draw.\&
.PP
How to play:
.IP \(bu 2
\%Tic\-tac\-toe is not on \%LIST \%GAMES \%(wopr \%\-\-games shows it under
\%ALSO \%AVAILABLE).\&
Start it with \%wopr \%ttt, or ask \%WOPR for it by name.\&
.IP \(bu 2
At \%PLEASE \%LIST \%NUMBER \%OF \%PLAYERS: type 1 to play \%WOPR.\&
Type 2 for two people taking turns at one keyboard.\&
Type 0 to let \%WOPR play itself, as at the end of the film.\&
.IP \(bu 2
With one player you are X and move first.\&
\%WOPR is O and answers each move with a line such as \%WOPR: 3.\&
.IP \(bu 2
At \%YOUR \%MOVE: type a square from 1 to 9.\&
The squares are numbered left to right from the top row.\&
Or type a column letter and a row number, from \%A1 (top left) to \%C3 (bottom
right).\&
A taken square gets \%CHOOSE \%AN \%EMPTY \%SQUARE, 1 \%TO 9.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Three in a row wins.\&
A full board with no line is a draw: \%WINNER: \%NONE.\&
.PP
Tips:
.IP \(bu 2
\%WOPR never makes a mistake, so a draw is the best result.\&
Before each move, check whether \%WOPR has two in a row with the third square
empty.\&
If it has, block that square.\&
.IP \(bu 2
Open in a corner.\&
The only safe answer is the centre.\&
\%WOPR always finds it, but a friend playing O in a \%two\-player game often
does not.\&
.IP \(bu 2
\%WOPR picks at random among its best moves, so games differ even though it
never loses.\&
With a seed \%(wopr \%ttt \%\-s 7), it makes the same choices every time.\&
That helps you study a line of play.\&
.SH "MOVIE SCENES"
\%\fBwopr\fR \%\fB\-\-movie\fR replays the film's scenes at \%WOPR's terminal,
typed and paced as on screen, through the same console and games you play
with.\&
These are the scenes, in the film's order:
.TP
.B "1 \%first\-contact"
\%LOGON \%ATTEMPTS, \%HELP \%AND \%THE \%LIST \%OF \%GAMES
.TP
.B "2 \%joshua"
\%THE \%BACKDOOR, \%THE \%GREETING \%AND A \%GAME \%OF \%CHOICE
.TP
.B "3 \%first\-strike"
A \%SIDE, \%TWO \%TARGETS \%AND \%THE \%BIG \%BOARD
.TP
.B "4 \%call\-back"
\%WOPR \%CALLS \%BACK \%TO \%FINISH \%THE \%GAME
.TP
.B "5 \%norad\-terminal"
\%JOSHUA \%AT \%NORAD: \%KILL \%RATIOS \%AND \%FALKEN'S \%ADDRESS
.TP
.B "6 \%climax"
\%DEFCON 1, \%TIC\-TAC\-TOE \%AND A \%STRANGE \%GAME
.PP
\%\fBwopr\fR \%\fB\-m\fR \fB3\fR, or \%\fBwopr\fR \%\fB\-m\fR
\%\fBfirst\-strike\fR, or any start of a name that fits one scene, plays from
that scene to the end, then exits.\&
With no scene, \%\fBwopr\fR \%\fB\-m\fR opens a menu of the scenes.\&
Add \%\fB\-o\fR \%(\fB\-\-only\fR) to play just one scene.\&
\%\fBwopr\fR \%\fB\-\-scenes\fR lists the scenes.\&
.PP
While a scene plays, Space pauses and resumes. \%\fBn\fR or Right skips to the
next scene. \%\fBp\fR or Left goes back one scene.\&
Esc opens the menu.\&
Once the \%climax reaches \%tic\-tac\-toe, it plays to the end.\&
Every replay is the same: movie mode ignores \%\fB\-\-seed\fR and
\%\fBWOPR_SEED\fR.\&
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The session ended normally: \%\fBLOGOFF\fR, the end of movie mode, a
\%print\-and\-exit option, or \%SIGTERM.\&
.TP
.B 1
A runtime error or a crash.\&
.TP
.B 2
A usage error.\&
The command line has an unknown option, game, scene or theme, a seed that is not
a whole number, or too many arguments.\&
Or it combines options that cannot go together, or it names something that fits
more than one.\&
Or there is no terminal to run in: standard output is not a terminal, or
\%\fBTERM\fR is \%\fBdumb\fR.\&
.TP
.B 130
\%Ctrl+C or \%SIGINT.\&
.SH ENVIRONMENT
A variable that switches something on is false when it is empty or 0, false, no
or off, and true otherwise.\&
.TP
.B \%WOPR_THEME
It gives \%\fB\-\-theme\fR its value \%(imsai, \%green, \%amber, \%norad).\&
Empty is the same as unset.\&
.TP
.B \%WOPR_INSTANT
When true, it turns on \%\fB\-\-instant\fR (no typewriter pacing).\&
.TP
.B \%WOPR_SEED
It gives \%\fB\-\-seed\fR its value (a repeatable run with this seed).\&
Empty is the same as unset.\&
.TP
.B \%WOPR_REDUCE_MOTION
When true, it turns on \%\fB\-\-reduce\-motion\fR (no blinking or
\%speed\-ups).\&
.TP
.B \%WOPR_PANEL
When true, it shows the front panel in any theme.\&
When false, it hides the panel in any theme.\&
When it is unset or empty, the theme decides: \%\fBnorad\fR shows the panel.\&
.TP
.B \%WOPR_DEBUG
When true, \%\fBwopr\fR writes a debug log (never what you type) and prints its
path when it exits.\&
See \%FILES.\&
.TP
.B \%NO_COLOR
When it is set to any value but an empty one, it turns colour off.\&
.TP
.B \%TMUX
tmux sets it.\&
When it is set, \%\fBwopr\fR draws in \%24\-bit colour, and tmux changes the
colours for a terminal that cannot show them.\&
\%\fBwopr\fR removes it from its environment when it starts, so that no library
runs \%\fBtmux\fR.\&
.TP
.B \%TERM
When it is \%\fBdumb\fR, \%\fBwopr\fR does not start its \%full\-screen
interface, and it exits with status 2.\&
The options that print and exit still work.\&
\%\fBwopr\fR ignores a value that is not a terminal's name, such as a file
path.\&
.SH FILES
.TP
.I \%\(ti/.cache/wopr/debug.log
The debug log that \%\fBWOPR_DEBUG\fR turns on.\&
It is under \%\fI$XDG_CACHE_HOME\fR when that is set, and in
\%\fI\(ti/Library/Caches/wopr\fR on \%macOS.\&
.SH NOTES
\%\fBwopr\fR is a \%fan\-made homage.\&
It is not affiliated with or endorsed by the makers or owners of
\%\fIWarGames\fR.\&
The code is under the \%MIT licence, which does not cover the film quotations
and the \%third\-party text.\&
\%\fBwopr\fR \%\fB\-\-licenses\fR prints the licence, \%\fINOTICE.md\fR, which
says what they are and who they belong to, and the licences of the
\%third\-party code built into \%\fBwopr\fR.\&
.SH BUGS
Report bugs in the issue tracker: \%https://github.com/GhostofGoes/WOPR/issues
.PP
Run \%\fBwopr\fR with \%\fBWOPR_DEBUG=1\fR.\&
Attach the debug log that it names when it exits, and the seed if you used
one.\&
Nobody has tried the \%full\-screen interface with a screen reader yet.\&
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B \%wopr
Dial in and log on.\&
.TP
.B "\%wopr \%chess \%\-i"
Play \%chess with no typewriter pacing.\&
.TP
.B "\%wopr \%\-\-games"
List the games.\&
.TP
.B "\%wopr \%\-m 2"
Replay the film from scene 2.\&
.TP
.B "\%wopr \%\-m \%joshua \%\-o"
Replay just the \%\fBjoshua\fR scene.\&
.TP
.B "\%WOPR_THEME=green \%wopr \%\-r"
Use the \%\fBgreen\fR theme, with no blinking.\&
